when can I go to the stars one day by  sweet what will be there anything you  can imagine our galaxy has billions of  stars each of those stars could have  many worlds every world could be home to  a different form of life and every life  is a special story of its own these  words spoken by astronaut Buzz Aldrin  and Mass Effect 3 post-credits team  encapsulate what I think makes science  fiction so great it's the idea that our  storytelling is limited only by the  reach of our imagination  the notion that there will always be  room for one more myth or one more  legend in places far far away the  characters of the Mass Effect trilogy  have become such icon their saga has  elevated them above video game stardom  they've transcended the medium and  become part of a wider pop culture but  now Mass Effect Andromeda is in our  hands it takes us to a whole new galaxy  and presents us with another world of  storytelling potential but it squandered  that potential viewed through the wider  scope of science fiction  Andromeda is a giant leap in the wrong  direction  [Music]  you  for as long as we've been around humans  have told stories there on the cave  walls of modern-day France they covered  papyrus in the Library of Alexandria  they've been passed down from generation  to generation around campfires and  dinner tables morphing with each new  telling and look also at the evolution  of our technology  the printing press brought mass media  the Kinetoscope brought early film  computer circuitry led to animation the  internet and of course videogames we've  always thought news stories yes but also  new ways to tell those stories science  fiction has played an important part in  that process it grants us an escape from  reality where we can see ourselves in  different worlds with different rule  sets it connected a sort of test chamber  or as one of my co-workers recently put  it a petri dish in other words the best  stories in the genre offered insight  into the human condition the author  Jules Verne offered such captivating  sci-fi stories that literary critics  were all but forced to take the genre  seriously work such as 20,000 Leagues  Under the Sea  around the world in 80 days and journey  to the center of the earth had profound  impact on both the literary and the  scientific world the author Ray Bradbury  even went so far as to say we are all in  one way or another the children of Jules  Verne's during the 20th century  so IFI expanded into other fields as  well inspired by several of Verne's  novel French director George Malley A's  released a trip to the moon in 1902 it  was the earliest example of sci-fi and  cinema Fritz Lang's Metropolis widely  considered the first great  feature-length sci-fi film shows us the  city where dictators control the  populace through robotic sutter fuge and  paranoia it's setting doesn't just serve  an aesthetic purpose but a functional  one as well it uses its science fiction  to show the class struggle between the  city's poor inhabitants and upper-class  elite in 1938 the public expressed  widespread outrage after hearing the War  of the Worlds radio broadcast in which  Orson Welles presented an alien invasion  as a series of realistic news bulletins  national media decried the approach  calling it deceptive and its delivery to  the masses it blurs the lines between  fiction and reality the narrative showed  the hysteria and confusion that might  come along with extraterrestrial contact  even more classics emerge throughout the  century each of them offering a  different take on the sci-fi genre their  own distinct ways of telling new stories  2001 a Space Odyssey further  cemented stanley kubrick's genius ridley  scott became a household name with a  lien and Blade Runner perhaps one of the  best films exploring what it means to be  human  then came the matrix the movie that  offered a postmodern look at our  perception of reality and of course we  have Star Wars it's the defining space  opera in one of America's biggest  contributions to pop culture it spawned  TV series novels and teen fiction  national fan conventions and a four-day  annual celebration it's a testament to  how pervasive science fiction has become  but it's not the plots of all these  films that we've grown attached to it's  usually the character names such as  Ellen Ripley Darth Vader neo forever  ingrained in our collective memory  common people who when thrust into  otherworldly situations become heroes  villains or gods along the way it comes  as no surprise then that science fiction  made its way into video games it's a  medium that thrives on escapism and when  combined with the mystery and wonder of  sci-fi that thrill multiplies tenfold  first person shooters pit us against  intergalactic threats strategy titles  grants us control of alien species  horror games challenge us with the  unknown in the dark places of distant  space but in this medium more than  others we can roleplay the heroes and  make our own allies in front of villains  ourselves we can explore distant worlds  with an agency not allowed in other  forms of entertainment maybe that's why  the Mass Effect trilogy rests  comfortably at the pinnacle of sci-fi  video games  [Music]  building on its success with Knights of  the Old Republic developer Bioware  envelops us in a fully realized sci-fi  setting  we're not witnessing the first contact  between humans and aliens as so many  stories explored were dropped into a  milky way where humans have not only  accepted the presence of alien life but  grown accustomed to it the galaxy's  inhabitants have created a Galactic  government they've studied each other's  cultures they've shared technologies to  help or hinder the advancements of their  neighbors which is all to say that Mass  Effect understands what makes sci-fi  great to begin with and wields those  strengths was undeniable force it  acknowledges the difference between  alien races they create characters that  embody and celebrate those differences  they have habits an idiosyncrasy so when  they do do something out of character  it's all the more endearing how are you  getting drunk fairly carefully Tory and  brandy triple filtered then introduced  into the suit through an emergency  induction park that's a straw Ellie say  what you will about Mass Effect 3  controversial ending but the journey  there is filled with moments of mythical  proportions the trilogy takes a diverse  galaxy on the brink of turmoil and uses  its characters to propel the narrative  at almost every turn  that's why Mass Effect Andromeda is all  the more disappointing it's a science  fiction game with massive potential that  it never fully realizes that potential  stems from its tantalizing premise  several groups from the Milky Way  including humans have launched the  Andromeda initiative and attempt to find  habitable planets in the neighboring yet  distant andromeda galaxy  in this case we're the aliens visiting  from a distant world this presents a  slew of narrative opportunities does  this galaxy have sentient life how will  we communicate with them what kind of  wonders or dangers will we encounter  what difficult choices will come with  the burden of our racist survival but  rather than Chaffey storylines any  meaningful direction Andromeda seems  content to guide us through stilted  dialogue boring side quests and lifeless  world this galaxy is gorgeous yes  but there's not much personality here  it's all spectacle and no soul this  should be a story about character  struggling to build a new home after  they've left everything they know behind  for the first several hours Andromeda  characters make only passing comments  about  fact this game constantly tells us that  things are dire but it doesn't Excel  it's showing us as the hours passed we  meet new alien races discover their  motivations and explore new planets that  may or may not be a new home for  Humanity food is scarce and supplies are  dwindling this is a race against time  this should be intense but it doesn't  feel intense we spend hours scanning the  environment and performing menial tasks  we speak to characters with fascinating  backstories but lack luster personality  few things we do create the wonder or  dread that the game tells us we are  supposed to be stealing look at Mass  Effect 2 on the other hand here's a game  that builds expertly torn operation so  daunting so seemingly impossible that  its dubbed the suicide mission only by  gaining your squadmates loyalty and  learning their individual strengths and  weaknesses can you emerge unscathed as a  team and in Mass Effect 2 this process  is thrilling dialogue is captivating  missions carry weight these characters  are painfully human they're lonely yet  strong terrified but brave as you  uncover more of Bioware spellbinding  galaxies you develop very real  attachments to the team  not sure if Torian hasn't is the same as  you all are spun this thing goes  sideways and we both end up down meet me  at the boss I'm by what Mass Effect  Andromeda doesn't deliver the same  experience if characters take too long  to captivate if galaxy is too boring to  pull me through there is a good sci-fi  tale lurking somewhere under the surface  but it's buried by tedium and  frustration an interesting premise is  one thing a well told story is another  mess Effect Andromeda reaches for the  ladder but Falls considerably short yes  it has its shining moments and I'm  hopeful Bioware Montreal can remember  them in any sequel the studio makes but  overall Andromeda remains a  disappointing sci-fi story it lacks the  imagination of the Titans that came  before it it lacks the heart that makes  the best ones memorable  yet if there's anything history tells us  it's that there's always hope for  another great sci-fi adventure as Buzz  Aldrin says in Mass Effect 3 looking up  at that starry sky there are billions of  stars left to explore  [M